{"id":"neet-2026-q002","pn":2,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter","st":"Wave and particle nature of light","q":"Match List I with List II :\nList I — List II\nA. E = hν — I. de Broglie wavelength\nB. Diffraction and Interference — II. Particle nature of light\nC. λ = h/p — III. Wave nature of light\nD. Compton effect — IV. Energy of photon\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"c":0,"s":"A. E = hν is the energy of a photon.\nB. Diffraction and interference show the wave nature of light.\nC. λ = h/p is the de Broglie wavelength of a particle.\nD. The Compton effect shows the particle nature of light.\nSo the match is A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q014","pn":14,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter","st":"Photoelectric effect graphs","q":"A beam of light falls on a metal surface such that photo-electrons are generated. If power of the light source starts to decrease linearly with time t, then variation of the photocurrent I and magnitude of the stopping potential |V| with time is best represented by:","o":["I decreases linearly with t (straight line falling to zero); |V| decreases linearly with t","I is constant with t; |V| decreases linearly with t","I is constant with t; |V| is constant with t","I decreases linearly with t; |V| is constant with t"],"c":3,"s":"Power of the source decreases, so the number of photons per second decreases linearly with time. The source is the same, so the energy of each photon is the same.\nPower P ∝ nE, so P ∝ n (number of photons per second).\nSo photocurrent I decreases linearly with time.\nPhoton energy is constant, so the stopping potential |V| stays constant with time.","fig":{"w":440,"h":195,"alt":"Four options, each a pair of graphs of photocurrent I and stopping potential |V| against time t: (1) both fall linearly, (2) I constant and |V| falls linearly, (3) both constant, (4) I falls linearly and |V| is constant.","d":[["t",2,44,"(1)"],["l",42,70,42,8,"a"],["l",42,70,130,70,"a"],["l",42,20,97,70,""],["t",36,25,"I","e s"],["t",82,83,"t","m s"],["l",142,70,142,8,"a"],["l",142,70,230,70,"a"],["l",142,20,197,70,""],["t",136,25,"|V|","e s"],["t",182,83,"t","m s"],["t",212,44,"(2)"],["l",252,70,252,8,"a"],["l",252,70,340,70,"a"],["l",252,20,307,20,""],["t",246,25,"I","e s"],["t",292,83,"t","m s"],["l",352,70,352,8,"a"],["l",352,70,440,70,"a"],["l",352,20,407,70,""],["t",346,25,"|V|","e s"],["t",392,83,"t","m s"],["t",2,144,"(3)"],["l",42,170,42,108,"a"],["l",42,170,130,170,"a"],["l",42,120,97,120,""],["t",36,125,"I","e s"],["t",82,183,"t","m s"],["l",142,170,142,108,"a"],["l",142,170,230,170,"a"],["l",142,120,197,120,""],["t",136,125,"|V|","e s"],["t",182,183,"t","m s"],["t",212,144,"(4)"],["l",252,170,252,108,"a"],["l",252,170,340,170,"a"],["l",252,120,307,170,""],["t",246,125,"I","e s"],["t",292,183,"t","m s"],["l",352,170,352,108,"a"],["l",352,170,440,170,"a"],["l",352,120,407,120,""],["t",346,125,"|V|","e s"],["t",392,183,"t","m s"]]},"sfig":{"w":260,"h":195,"alt":"I versus t is a straight line falling to zero; |V| versus t is a horizontal line.","d":[["l",30,80,30,15,"a"],["l",30,80,105,80,"a"],["l",30,28,80,80],["t",24,24,"I","e s"],["t",65,95,"t","m s"],["l",30,165,30,100,"a"],["l",30,165,105,165,"a"],["l",30,112,80,112],["t",24,108,"|V|","e s"],["t",65,180,"t","m s"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q023","pn":23,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter","st":"Photoelectric cells stopping potential","q":"A ray of light with wavelength λ is incident on three different photoelectric cells namely 1, 2 and 3. The threshold wavelength of these photoelectric cells are λ₁, λ₂, and λ₃, respectively and the magnitude of stopping potentials of these cells are V₁, V₂ and V₃, respectively. The relation between λ and threshold wavelengths are λ₁ < λ, λ₂ > λ and λ₃ >> λ. The correct option is:","o":["V₁ = 0, V₂ > V₃","V₁ > V₂, V₃ = 0","V₁ < V₂, V₃ = 0","V₁ = 0, V₂ < V₃"],"c":3,"s":"An electron is ejected only if the photon energy is greater than the work function, φ = hc/λ.\nFor cell 1, λ₁ < λ, so φ₁ > φ (work function of cell 1 is greater than the photon energy).\nSo no electron is ejected and V₁ = 0.\nEinstein equation: photon energy φ = φ₀ + eV.\nFor cells 2 and 3: λ₂ > λ and λ₃ >> λ, so φ > φ₂ and φ >> φ₃ (work functions are smaller than photon energy).\nSo V₃ > V₂."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q040","pn":40,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter","st":"Momentum of photon and electron","q":"A photon and an electron, each of 20 eV energy, move in free space. The ratio of linear momentum of electron pₑ to that of photon pPh, pₑ/pPh is:\n[Take speed of light = 3 × 10⁸ ms⁻¹, charge of electron = –1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C and mass of electron = 9 × 10⁻³¹ kg]","o":["1/250","225","275","2/450"],"c":1,"s":"Eₑ = EPh = 20 eV.\nFor the electron pₑ = √(2mₑEₑ), for the photon pPh = EPh/c.\npₑ/pPh = (√(2mₑEₑ)/EPh)·c = √(2mₑ/EPh)·c.\npₑ/pPh = √((2 × 9 × 10⁻³¹)/(20 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹)) × 3 × 10⁸.\n= (3/4) × 10⁻⁶ × 3 × 10⁸.\n= 900/4 = 225."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q042","pn":42,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter","st":"Photoelectric effect","q":"For a metal of work function 6.6 eV, which of the following wavelengths of incident radiation does not give rise to the photoelectric effect?\n(Take Planck's constant as 6.6×10⁻³⁴ J s)","o":["200 nm","150 nm","100 nm","50 nm"],"c":0,"s":"The photoelectric effect does not occur when hc/λ < work function.\nThat means λ > hc/W₀.\nλ > (6.6×10⁻³⁴ × 3×10⁸)/(6.6 × 1.6×10⁻¹⁹).\nλ > (3×10⁻⁷)/1.6 m = 300/1.6 nm.\nλ > 187.5 nm.\nSo 200 nm is the wavelength with no photoelectric effect."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q037","pn":37,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter","st":"de Broglie wavelength","q":"A photon and an electron (mass m) have the same energy E. The ratio (λ_photon/λ_electron) of their de Broglie wavelengths is: (c is the speed of light)","o":["√(E/2m)","c√(2mE)","c√(2m/E)","(1/c)√(E/2m)"],"c":2,"s":"For the photon, E = hc/λ_Ph, so λ_Ph = hc/E.\nFor the electron, p is the momentum and E = p²/2m = (h/λ_e)² × 1/(2m).\nSo λ_e = h/√(2mE).\nλ_Ph/λ_e = (hc/E)/(h/√(2mE)) = c√(2m/E)."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q038","pn":38,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter","st":"Photoelectric effect graphs","q":"Which of the following options represent the variation of photoelectric current with property of light shown on the x-axis?\nA. Graph of photoelectric current (y-axis) against Intensity of light (x-axis): a straight line rising from the origin.\nB. Photoelectric current against Intensity of light: a horizontal straight line (constant current).\nC. Photoelectric current against Frequency of light: a straight line rising from the origin.\nD. Photoelectric current against Frequency of light: a straight line starting on the x-axis at a positive frequency (not at the origin) and rising.","o":["A only","A and C","A and D","B and D"],"c":0,"s":"Photoelectric current is directly proportional to the intensity of light.\nSo the graph of current against intensity is a straight line through the origin (graph A).","fig":{"w":489.8,"h":272,"alt":"Four graphs of photoelectric current: A against intensity, a straight line from the origin; B against intensity, a horizontal line; C against frequency, a straight line from the origin; D against frequency, a line starting on the axis at a positive frequency","d":[["l",62,112.3,62,28.6,"a"],["l",62,112.3,173.6,112.3,"a"],["t",53.3,118.5,"0","s"],["t",69.4,130,"Intensity of light","s"],["t",65.7,23.6,"Photoelectric current","s"],["l",62,112.3,143.8,36,"B"],["t",5,79.4,"A."],["l",372.6,112.3,372.6,28.6,"a"],["l",372.6,112.3,483.6,112.3,"a"],["t",363.9,118.5,"0","s"],["t",380.1,130,"Intensity of light","s"],["t",376.3,23.6,"Photoelectric current","s"],["l",372.6,49.7,476.8,49.7,"B"],["t",315.6,79.4,"B."],["l",62,234.8,62,153.6,"a"],["l",62,234.8,173.6,234.8,"a"],["t",53.3,241,"0","s"],["t",69.4,262.7,"Frequency of light","s"],["t",65.7,148.6,"Photoelectric current","s"],["l",62,234.8,156.9,160.4,"B"],["t",5,203.8,"C."],["l",372.6,234.8,372.6,153.6,"a"],["l",372.6,234.8,483.6,234.8,"a"],["t",363.9,241,"0","s"],["t",380.1,262.7,"Frequency of light","s"],["t",376.3,148.6,"Photoelectric current","s"],["l",403,234.8,484.2,162.9,"B"],["t",315.6,203.8,"D."]]},"sfig":{"w":220,"h":190,"alt":"Graph of photoelectric current against intensity of light: straight line through the origin","d":[["l",23,147,23,15,"a"],["l",23,147,202,147,"a"],["l",23,147,155,26,"B"],["t",35,175,"Intensity of light","s"],["t",32,10,"Photoelectric current","s"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2024-q011","pn":11,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter","st":"de Broglie wavelength and kinetic energy","q":"The graph which shows the variation of (1/λ²) and its kinetic energy, E is (where λ is de Broglie wavelength of a free particle)","o":["graph 1","graph 2","graph 3","graph 4"],"c":1,"s":"de Broglie wavelength λ = h/p = h/(mv) = h/√(2mE), where E = ½mv².\nSquaring both sides: λ² = h²/(2mE).\nSo 1/λ² = (2m/h²) E, which is a constant times E.\nThe graph is a straight line through the origin with constant slope.\nNote: The printed solution has λ² = h²/(4m²E), a misprint; the correct form is h²/(2mE). The conclusion is unchanged.","fig":{"w":320,"h":230,"alt":"Four graphs of 1/λ² against E: (1) rising concave-down curve from the origin, (2) straight line through the origin, (3) rising concave-up curve, (4) falling curve.","d":[["l",34,92,34,12,"a"],["l",34,92,136,92,"a"],["t",32,40,"1/λ²","e"],["t",28,105,"O","e"],["t",140,96,"E"],["t",2,80,"(1)"],["p","M34 92 C36 55 65 33 100 30",""],["l",199,92,199,12,"a"],["l",199,92,301,92,"a"],["t",197,40,"1/λ²","e"],["t",193,105,"O","e"],["t",305,96,"E"],["t",167,80,"(2)"],["p","M199 92 L270 30",""],["l",34,207,34,127,"a"],["l",34,207,136,207,"a"],["t",32,155,"1/λ²","e"],["t",28,220,"O","e"],["t",140,211,"E"],["t",2,195,"(3)"],["p","M34 207 C70 205 98 187 102 143",""],["l",199,207,199,127,"a"],["l",199,207,301,207,"a"],["t",197,155,"1/λ²","e"],["t",193,220,"O","e"],["t",305,211,"E"],["t",167,195,"(4)"],["p","M205 143 C207 185 235 203 290 205",""]]}},
{"id":"neet-2024-q020","pn":20,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter","st":"Photon properties","q":"If c is the velocity of light in free space, the correct statements about photon among the following are\nA. The energy of a photon is E = hν.\nB. The velocity of a photon is c.\nC. The momentum of a photon, p = hν/c.\nD. In a photon-electron collision, both total energy and total momentum are conserved.\nE. Photon possesses positive charge.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A, C and D only","A, B, D and E only","A and B only","A, B, C and D only"],"c":3,"s":"A: the energy of a photon is E = hν.\nB: a photon moves with the speed of light, c.\nC: λ = h/p, so p = h/λ = hν/c.\nD: in a photon-electron collision both total energy and total momentum are conserved.\nE is false: a photon has no charge.\nSo A, B, C and D are correct."}
